r['interpretation']+=("\n\nQ2 (outflow separation, round 2): the S blue lobe (365 pc S of S, PA 183-194 deg, v_los -100..-400 km/s) is separable from the nuclei and cannot be beam-smeared disk rotation (velocity gradient at the lobe 30-40 km/s/arcsec vs >=340 needed; null model predicts <0.01 vs 0.30 Jy km/s observed), and it lies where the CO outflow of Sakamoto+2014 and the HCN/HCO+(1-0) South-OF-Blue of Michiyama+2018 are; the nuclear wings at S and N, by contrast, are reproducible by beam-smeared nuclear rotation plus broad lines. HCN and HCO+ broad components have different kinematics (HCN bluer, broader), so the HCN/HCO+ ratio of the lobe is window dependent (1.5 in -405..-150, 0.6-0.9 over the whole broad component, 0.42 in the narrow component) and the HDO alternative for the HCN excess stays open. A non-LTE treatment (HCO+ mass 0.8-2.6e7 Msun for X(HCO+) 1e-8..3e-9, HCN-based 10x larger and requiring N(HCN)~10N(HCO+) or IR pumping) shows the dense mass is ~1e7 Msun, comparable with the CO outflow mass of the lobe (dense fraction 0.1-1, unconstrained), and Mdot_dense ~10-60 Msun/yr for a flow within 70-85 deg of the line of sight (any value from 3 to 2000 if all assumptions are varied), comparable to the CO rate (50 X20, 40-104) and to the SFR. The red feature 1.7\" S of N is a real, localised red-shifted clump/ridge at the position of Michiyama's OF-Red; it is not leakage and not a simple extension of the N wing, but its origin (S counter-lobe, N outflow, Bridge) is open.")
